-- 개념 인트로 UPDATE v2 — canon_slug 키 (2026-07-02)

update sm_concepts set intro = 'Repetition compulsion names Freud''s unsettling discovery that the psyche doesn''t just avoid pain — it actively restages it, returning to the same wound as if repetition itself might finally master what the original event could not process. Cinema is practically built for this logic: the character who keeps choosing the same doomed lover, the loop structure that traps a protagonist in identical days, the flashback that won''t stay in the past no matter how the plot tries to move forward. What makes the device so cinematic is that film can literalize the compulsion visually — repeating a shot, a line, a gesture — so the audience feels the trap the character can''t see. The films gathered below all stage some version of this return, and reading them together shows how differently directors treat repetition: as horror, as comedy, as the last, stubborn form of hope.' where canon_slug = 'repetition-compulsion';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s death drive is the idea that alongside the pleasure principle''s push toward gratification, there runs a countercurrent aimed at reduction, stillness, and ultimately the extinction of tension itself — a pull toward the inorganic that undercuts any simple story of self-preservation. On screen this rarely announces itself as literal suicide; it shows up as self-sabotage, as characters who court destruction with a fascination no rational motive explains, as narratives that seem to want their own ending more than any resolution. Genre cinema is especially haunted by it — the noir antihero walking toward the gun, the action film''s obsession with its own wreckage. The selection below traces this drive across wildly different registers, from the elegiac to the nihilistic, and asks what it means for a medium built on motion to keep circling back to stillness.' where canon_slug = 'death-drive';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kant''s Formula of Humanity insists that every rational being must be treated as an end in themselves, never merely as a means to someone else''s purpose — a moral floor beneath which no calculation of utility is allowed to sink. Film tests this principle constantly, often by breaking it: the heist plan that treats a bystander as expendable, the institution that processes people as data points, the moment a character finally refuses to be used and the story pivots around that refusal. Because the formula is so abstract, cinema''s job is to give it a face — a body reduced to instrument, a person who insists on being seen as more than their function. The films below approach that instrumentalization from different angles, and together they map where Kantian dignity survives on screen and where it quietly gets sacrificed.' where canon_slug = 'the-formula-of-humanity';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Marx''s commodity fetishism describes how objects, once they enter exchange, seem to acquire a life and value of their own, obscuring the human labor that actually produced them — the social relation between people disguised as a relation between things. Cinema, itself a manufacturer of glossy, desirable images, is uncannily well positioned to expose or reproduce this trick: the product shot lingered over like a religious icon, the workplace scene that shows the hands behind the glamour, the character who falls in love with a thing rather than a person. Some of the films below indulge the fetish outright, letting objects glow with unearned meaning, while others strip the varnish off to reveal the labor and exploitation underneath. Read side by side, they trace cinema''s double role as both accomplice to and critic of the spell commodities cast.' where canon_slug = 'commodity-fetishism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Guy Debord''s spectacle names a stage of capitalism where authentic social life is displaced by representation — where we no longer live events so much as consume their mediated image, and where even rebellion risks being absorbed back into the show. Film is not innocent here; it is one of the spectacle''s primary engines, which is what makes self-aware cinema about media, celebrity, and surveillance so charged — the movie that turns its camera on the cameras, the protagonist who can''t tell if they''re living their life or performing it. The strongest films on this theme don''t just depict spectacle, they implicate the viewer''s own hunger for it. Below is a set of films that stage this collapse of the real into the image, each testing how much distance, if any, cinema can keep from the spectacle it''s made of.' where canon_slug = 'the-society-of-the-spectacle';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'The return of the repressed is Freud''s account of how what gets pushed out of consciousness doesn''t disappear but waits, resurfacing in distorted form — a symptom, a slip, a face that shouldn''t be there. Horror and the uncanny thriller have practically made this their house style: the past crime that won''t stay buried, the family secret that erupts at exactly the wrong dinner, the double who embodies everything the protagonist has denied about themselves. What''s cinematic about the mechanism is its timing — repression works through delay, and film''s control over pacing and revelation lets directors engineer that delay with precision. The titles collected here show the repressed returning in registers from the domestic to the supernatural, and reading them together clarifies just how load-bearing this one psychoanalytic idea has become for genre storytelling.' where canon_slug = 'return-of-the-repressed';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Sartre''s bad faith is the peculiar self-deception of a free being who denies its own freedom, pretending to be a fixed thing — a role, a type, a victim of circumstance — rather than facing the anguish of having to choose. On screen it looks like the character who hides behind a job title, a social script, or an excuse, and the camera often catches the gap between what they claim they must do and what they''re actually choosing. Existentialist and post-war cinema in particular loves catching people in this posture, staging the moment bad faith cracks and someone has to own a decision they''d rather blame on fate. The films below stage that confrontation in different keys, from quiet domestic drama to outright farce, and together they show how hard — and how tempting — it is to stop performing necessity.' where canon_slug = 'bad-faith';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Hannah Arendt coined the banality of evil after watching Eichmann testify, struck not by monstrous malice but by his sheer thoughtlessness — evil enacted through bureaucratic routine, clichés, and a failure to think rather than demonic will. Cinema has had to invent new tools to picture this, because villainy on screen traditionally demands charisma or menace, and Arendt''s insight is that the most consequential evil often has neither: it fills out forms, follows orders, goes home for dinner. The films gathered below resist the temptation to make their perpetrators theatrical, instead lingering on paperwork, procedure, and the terrifying ordinariness of the people who carry atrocity out. Watching them in sequence makes the banality thesis concrete in a way the phrase alone never quite manages.' where canon_slug = 'the-banality-of-evil';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Where classical psychoanalysis treats the return of the repressed as an individual symptom, this related strand of the idea pushes the concept outward — asking what happens when an entire culture, genre, or history represses something collectively, only to have it resurface in myth, ritual, or the horror film itself. Robin Wood''s influential reworking of the idea for American horror argued that monsters are always the culture''s own disavowed content coming back dressed up as Other. The films below lean into that social register rather than the purely personal one, using the uncanny to smuggle back what a community, a nation, or an era would rather forget. Taken together they suggest that repression, like the past, is never merely private.' where canon_slug = 'the-return-of-the-repressed';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s Real is the register that resists symbolization entirely — not reality as we experience it, but the raw, traumatic kernel that language and image can only circle, never capture, the thing that ruptures the smooth fictions of the Symbolic and Imaginary. Film, so dependent on representation, has a strange relationship to this concept: its most Lacanian moments are often the ones where the image seems to break down, where a scene of violence or bodily excess refuses to be absorbed into narrative meaning. Directors chase this rupture through sound that overwhelms sense, images that linger past comfort, or plots that simply refuse coherent resolution. The selection below gathers films that brush up against this limit, each testing how close cinema can get to showing the unshowable before the frame itself gives way.' where canon_slug = 'the-real';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Henri Bergson''s durée names lived time as we actually experience it — qualitative, flowing, and irreducible to the discrete, measurable clock-time that science and habit impose on us, a continuous becoming rather than a row of separate instants. Deleuze later made Bergson central to film theory precisely because cinema, through the long take, the dissolve, and rhythmic editing, can render duration in a way no other medium can, letting a shot breathe past narrative necessity into pure felt time. Slow cinema in particular treats durée as its whole aesthetic project, asking viewers to abandon clock-time and sink into a scene''s own tempo. The films below range widely in style but share this commitment to time as something felt rather than counted, and watching them back to back is itself an exercise in durée.' where canon_slug = 'duree';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Edward Said''s Orientalism describes the West''s long habit of constructing "the East" as a fantasy object — exotic, timeless, irrational, and feminized — a discourse that says less about the actual region depicted than about the anxieties and desires of the culture doing the depicting. Cinema has been one of Orientalism''s most powerful delivery systems, from the harem fantasy to the terrorist stereotype, dressing colonial assumptions up as adventure, romance, or thriller. But the films below aren''t only examples to condemn — some interrogate the gaze from inside it, using the camera''s own orientalizing habits against themselves. Read together, they trace both the machinery of the stereotype and the counter-images that have tried to dismantle it.' where canon_slug = 'orientalism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s objet petit a is the unattainable object-cause of desire — not the thing we consciously want, but the structural gap around which our wanting is organized, meaning that getting the object never satisfies because the object was never really the point. Film narrative runs on this logic more than most art forms: the MacGuffin nobody quite remembers, the beloved who becomes hollow the instant they''re finally attained, the ending that delivers the goal and somehow leaves the character emptier. The strongest films built around this idea understand that the chase, not the catch, is the actual subject. Below is a set of films that stage desire''s structural impossibility in different genres, each showing what happens to a story once its object turns out to be an empty placeholder.' where canon_slug = 'objet-petit-a';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s melancholia is a pathological form of mourning in which the lost object is not properly relinquished but internalized, so that the ego turns the aggression and grief it can''t express outward into a persistent, self-devouring identification with the loss. On screen this often produces a very specific tone — not the cathartic sob of tragedy but a flat, suspended sadness, a character who seems to be living inside a wound rather than working through it. Directors drawn to melancholia tend to favor stillness and repetition over dramatic release, letting grief calcify into atmosphere rather than event. The films collected here inhabit that register, and set beside more conventional mourning narratives, they show how differently cinema can render loss when it refuses to let the loss end.' where canon_slug = 'melancholia';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Julia Kristeva''s abject is what the self must expel to become a self at all — the corpse, the wound, the bodily fluid, the thing that blurs the line between subject and object, inside and outside, life and death, and so provokes a horror that is also, disturbingly, a kind of fascination. Horror cinema is the abject''s natural home, thriving on images the body would rather disavow — decay, contamination, the maternal body itself — because the genre''s pleasure is precisely this flirtation with the border Kristeva says we can never fully secure. What makes the concept useful beyond straightforward horror is how it explains disgust as a psychic event, not just a visual one. The films below push toward that border in different ways, and together they map how cinema turns revulsion into meaning.' where canon_slug = 'the-abject';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bertolt Brecht''s Verfremdungseffekt, the alienation or estrangement effect, aims to break the audience''s emotional absorption so they watch critically rather than empathetically — actors addressing the camera, artifice left visible, familiar situations made strange enough to provoke thought instead of identification. Film has borrowed this theatrical strategy wherever it wants to resist the seductive realism the medium defaults to: the fourth wall broken mid-scene, the deliberately unconvincing set, the narrator who undercuts the story being told. What''s striking is how many different political and comic uses directors have found for the same basic gesture of interruption. The films gathered below all pull this trick in some form, and comparing them shows how estrangement can serve satire, Marxist critique, or simple formal playfulness.' where canon_slug = 'verfremdungseffekt';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s uncanny, das Unheimliche, is the dread that arises when something long familiar becomes strange, when the homely reveals itself as also, somehow, unhomely — the doll that might be alive, the double, the house that is definitely, impossibly, not quite right. Cinema exploits this by working at the edge of recognition: the face slightly too still, the domestic space subtly wrong, repetition that starts to feel less like coincidence than design. Unlike outright horror, the uncanny thrives on ambiguity — it needs viewers unsure whether what they''re seeing is explicable or not. The films below linger in that uncertainty rather than resolving it, and taken as a set they demonstrate how much dread can be generated from the merely displaced ordinary.' where canon_slug = 'the-uncanny';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'In the essay that gives this concept its name, Freud distinguishes ordinary mourning, which eventually lets the lost object go, from melancholia, where the loss is absorbed into the ego itself and grief curdles into self-reproach with no clear end point. Cinema dealing with mourning proper tends to follow an arc — denial, breakdown, some form of release — while films drawn to the melancholic side of Freud''s essay resist that arc, showing grief as a structure a character inhabits rather than passes through. The interest of pairing the two states on screen is watching directors choose, often without naming it, which side of Freud''s distinction they''re filming. The selection below includes both trajectories, and reading them against each other clarifies where healthy grief ends and melancholic stasis begins.' where canon_slug = 'mourning-and-melancholia';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Giorgio Agamben''s bare life, zoē stripped of any political qualification, names the condition of being included in the political order only through exclusion — a life the sovereign can extinguish without it counting as murder, exemplified by the camp, the refugee, the stateless body suspended outside law''s normal protections. Film renders this through images of waiting, of bodies processed by systems that have already decided they don''t fully count — checkpoints, detention centers, the endless queue. What Agamben''s frame adds to these images is the recognition that this exclusion is not a breakdown of the political order but constitutive of it. The films below stage bare life in settings from the historical to the speculative, and together they ask where, exactly, the line of the sovereign''s protection actually falls.' where canon_slug = 'bare-life';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Marc Augé''s non-place describes the airports, highways, chain hotels, and transit zones of supermodernity — spaces that generate no relational identity, no history, no sense of belonging, only passage, and that leave the people moving through them anonymous even to themselves. Cinema has become fluent in this aesthetic, using the sterile glow of the departure lounge or the identical hotel corridor as a shorthand for alienation, dislocation, and the erosion of a stable self. What''s compelling is how differently films treat the non-place emotionally — some find it purely desolate, others locate an odd freedom in its anonymity. The films collected below all stage significant scenes inside these placeless places, and comparing them reveals two very different readings of the same architecture.' where canon_slug = 'non-place';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Nietzsche''s eternal recurrence poses a thought experiment as an ethical test: what if you had to live this exact life, in every detail, over and over for eternity — could you affirm it, or would the thought crush you? Film adapts this less as literal cosmology than as structure, using loop narratives and cyclical time to force characters to confront whether their life, repeated infinitely, is one they could actually want. The device works because repetition on screen isn''t abstract — we watch the same day, the same choices, replay with tiny variations, and feel the weight of the question alongside the character. The films below stage this test in genres from comedy to war film, and each asks, in its own way, whether affirmation is even possible once you''ve seen the whole loop.' where canon_slug = 'eternal-recurrence';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Emmanuel Levinas argues that the face of the Other makes an ethical demand prior to any rule or calculation — encountering another''s face, in its vulnerability, obligates me before I can reason my way out of it, grounding ethics not in principle but in this primary, inescapable exposure. Cinema, a medium organized around the close-up, is perhaps uniquely suited to stage this encounter, since the face-filling frame can force a viewer into exactly the kind of confrontation Levinas describes, refusing to let another person be reduced to background or type. The films below use the close-up and sustained eye contact deliberately, often at moments the plot could easily have skipped past, to make this ethical claim visible. Watching them together shows how much philosophical weight a single held shot of a face can carry.' where canon_slug = 'the-face-of-the-other';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Heidegger''s being-toward-death holds that authentic existence requires facing one''s own mortality directly, not as a distant fact but as a possibility always already structuring the present — only by owning this finitude can Dasein live authentically rather than dissolving into the anonymous chatter of "the they." Films drawn to this idea tend to stage a character''s confrontation with a concrete deadline or diagnosis, using it not for melodrama but to strip away the evasions that structure ordinary life, revealing what was actually urgent all along. The device works because cinema can compress a lifetime of avoidance into the space of a single revelatory sequence. The films below take up mortality as this kind of clarifying pressure, and together they trace what authentic living looks like once denial is no longer an option.' where canon_slug = 'being-toward-death';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s disavowal describes a peculiar split response to an unbearable perception — not repression''s outright burial, but a simultaneous knowing and refusing to know, holding two contradictory beliefs at once ("I know very well, but nevertheless..."). Film characters caught in disavowal tend to act on information they''ve technically registered while behaving as though they haven''t, and directors often stage this through a visual doubling — a character looking directly at evidence and somehow not seeing it. The concept also became central to film theory itself, describing how spectators disavow the artifice of cinema to stay immersed in it. The films below dramatize disavowal at the level of plot, and reading them alongside that theoretical history shows how the mechanism operates both inside the story and in the audience watching it.' where canon_slug = 'disavowal';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Adorno and Horkheimer''s culture industry names the way mass entertainment, produced along industrial lines, manufactures the appearance of individual choice while actually standardizing taste, pacifying dissent, and turning art into another interchangeable commodity. It''s a bracing lens to apply to film precisely because cinema is one of the culture industry''s flagship products, which makes films that turn a critical eye on entertainment itself, on fame, on the studio system, unusually self-implicating. The sharpest examples don''t just illustrate the thesis, they let the machinery show through the polish, exposing formula as formula. The films below examine mass culture from inside its own apparatus, and taken together they test how much critique a commercial medium can actually sustain against itself.' where canon_slug = 'the-culture-industry';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kant''s categorical imperative demands that we act only according to a maxim we could will to become universal law, replacing consequence-based reasoning with a test of consistency and duty that holds regardless of outcome or desire. Films built around this idea tend to stage moral dilemmas where doing the "right" universal thing produces a bad or even tragic result, forcing viewers to feel the tension between Kantian duty and the messier calculus of consequences. That friction is where the drama lives — the character who follows principle off a cliff, the one who breaks it and can''t live with the exception they made. The films below stage that collision in different contexts, and together they pressure-test how far duty-based ethics can travel once real stakes and real people are involved.' where canon_slug = 'the-categorical-imperative';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Achille Mbembe''s necropolitics extends Foucault''s biopower into the colony and its afterlives, describing how sovereignty is increasingly exercised not by fostering life but by administering death — deciding, through zones like the plantation, the checkpoint, or the refugee camp, which populations are exposed to war, disease, and slow dying. Film applies this lens most visibly in war stories and border narratives, where certain bodies are staged as already-dying long before the plot gets around to killing them, and the camera''s willingness or refusal to witness that death becomes its own political statement. Mbembe''s frame shifts the question away from who wins a given conflict and toward whose death was ever going to count as visible. The films below stage exactly that unevenness, and set side by side, they expose how consistently cinema sorts bodies into the seen and the disposable.' where canon_slug = 'necropolitics';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'The ethics of care, developed by thinkers like Carol Gilligan and Nel Noddings in response to abstract, rule-based moral philosophy, grounds ethics instead in relationship, attentiveness, and responsiveness to the concrete needs of particular others rather than in universal principle. Film versions of this ethic tend to look undramatic by design — the extended scene of feeding, tending, staying — because the moral weight lands in sustained attention rather than a single decisive act. What makes these films quietly radical is their refusal to treat care work as background to the "real" plot, insisting instead that the caring itself is the story. The films below foreground relationships of dependency and tending that most cinema treats as incidental, and together they argue for care as a fully sufficient ethical center.' where canon_slug = 'ethics-of-care';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Levinas pushes the encounter with the face of the Other further than a general ethical claim: the face imposes infinite responsibility, an obligation that precedes any contract, cannot be discharged by a single act of kindness, and only grows the more it''s answered — responsibility without ceiling or reciprocity. Where a film might use the close-up simply to establish ethical contact, this stronger version of the idea appears in narratives where a character''s responsibility to another keeps expanding past what any reasonable ethics would demand, refusing the comfort of a completed duty. The films below track that escalation specifically, distinguishing themselves from more general face-to-face encounters by showing responsibility that never resolves into "enough." Watched as a set, they push Levinas''s asymmetry to its most demanding, least comfortable conclusion.' where canon_slug = 'the-face-of-the-other-infinite-responsibility';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'René Girard''s scapegoat mechanism explains how communities in crisis, gripped by mimetic rivalry that threatens to become universal violence, discharge that violence onto a single victim whose expulsion or death restores unity — a sacrifice the group must believe is deserved for the mechanism to work at all. Film is an ideal stage for exposing this process because it can show both sides at once: the mounting social panic and the crowd''s conviction, followed by an ending that lets the audience see what the community couldn''t, that the victim was innocent all along. The most effective scapegoat films implicate the viewer too, letting us feel the pull of consensus before revealing its cost. The films below trace this arc from mounting crisis to sacrificial resolution, and together they make Girard''s uncomfortable claim about the origins of social order startlingly legible.' where canon_slug = 'the-scapegoat-mechanism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Before capitalism can run on wage labor, something has to be stolen first — Marx''s primitive accumulation names the violent prehistory of enclosure, expropriation, and dispossession that manufactures a propertyless class with nothing to sell but its labor. Cinema keeps returning to this originary theft: land seizures, company towns, colonial plantations, the frontier violence that clears the ground for a "legitimate" economy to later disavow its own founding crime. A film doesn''t need to cite Marx to stage the scene — any story where someone''s home becomes someone else''s capital is already doing the work. The reading list below traces how directors visualize the moment property gets born in blood.' where canon_slug = 'primitive-accumulation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Harvey''s update to Marx argues that dispossession never stayed in capitalism''s past — it''s a recurring mechanism, privatizing commons, foreclosing homes, and financializing crisis to transfer wealth upward again and again. On screen this shows up as the eviction notice, the pension fund gutted overnight, the neighborhood rezoned out from under its residents, each scene functioning as accumulation''s present tense rather than its origin story. What makes the concept useful for film analysis is its refusal of nostalgia: there is no innocent prior state being violated, only ongoing extraction dressed up as progress or recovery. These readings follow the camera into boardrooms and foreclosed kitchens alike to track dispossession as method, not accident.' where canon_slug = 'accumulation-by-dispossession';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s displacement describes how psychic energy detaches from a forbidden or dangerous object and reattaches to a safer, adjacent one, letting the dream (or the symptom, or the joke) say something without saying it. Film grammar is built for this kind of substitution — a camera lingers on a doorknob instead of a face, a minor character absorbs an anger that belongs to someone else entirely, and the audience feels the charge migrate before they can name its source. The trick works because meaning doesn''t need its original address to still deliver the letter. Below is a set of readings that follow displaced feeling across the frame to wherever it actually lands.' where canon_slug = 'displacement';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Agamben''s homo sacer is the figure the law produces by excluding him from its own protection — a life that can be killed without it counting as murder or sacrifice, included in the political order only as something exposed to its violence. Cinema stages bare life constantly: the stateless refugee, the detainee in indefinite limbo, the body stripped of any status but biological existence, filmed in spaces — camps, border zones, waiting rooms — that exist precisely to hold such figures outside ordinary law. What makes the concept sharp is that it isn''t marginal to sovereignty but foundational to it, the exception that reveals what the rule was protecting all along. The reading list traces this logic across films that turn the camera on exactly the people power has decided not to see.' where canon_slug = 'homo-sacer-bare-life';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Reaction-formation is Freud''s account of a defense that doesn''t just repress an unwanted impulse but flips it into its opposite, so that disgust masks desire and moral fervor masks the very appetite it condemns. Movies love this inversion because it''s visually legible — the crusading zealot whose fixation on sin betrays fascination, the homophobe whose violence reads as displaced longing, the prude whose fastidiousness protests too much. The pleasure for the viewer is dramatic irony: watching a character perform the negation of a wish while every gesture leaks the wish itself. These essays track that leak across performances built entirely on saying no too loudly.' where canon_slug = 'reaction-formation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kierkegaard''s phrase describes Abraham''s willingness to sacrifice Isaac — an act that ethics alone can only call murder, justified solely by a private relation to the absolute that exceeds any universal moral law. Film narrative is drawn to this exact fracture: the character who must betray every shared standard of right and wrong because a higher, unverifiable calling demands it, leaving the audience unable to adjudicate faith from madness. The unsettling part isn''t the violation of ethics but the total absence of any external proof that the suspension was warranted at all. The readings collected here follow protagonists into that unprovable leap and ask what the camera can and cannot show us about it.' where canon_slug = 'teleological-suspension-of-the-ethical';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Augé''s non-places are the anonymous, transient spaces of supermodernity — airports, motorways, hotel chains, shopping malls — where identity, relation, and history all fail to take root, leaving only instructions, screens, and passage. Films set in these zones often exploit their eerie interchangeability, letting a character disappear into an airport lounge that could be anywhere, or stage an entire emotional collapse against the flat hum of fluorescent non-space. The horror or comedy of it is the same: a place built for no one to belong to becomes exactly where belonging is tested. Below, a set of readings maps how cinema turns transit zones into stages for the modern self''s own placelessness.' where canon_slug = 'non-places';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Baudrillard''s simulacrum is a copy that has stopped referring to any original, and hyperreality is the condition where such copies feel more real than the reality they''ve replaced. Cinema, being itself a machine for manufacturing images without originals, is an obvious host for the idea — theme parks standing in for history, screens within screens, characters who can no longer distinguish the simulation from whatever it was supposed to simulate. The vertigo isn''t that the fake fools us, but that the question of fakeness stops making sense as a category at all. These readings follow films that dramatize a world where the map has fully replaced the territory.' where canon_slug = 'simulacrum-hyperreality';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Schmitt defined sovereignty as the power to decide the exception — the moment law is suspended in the name of an emergency that only the sovereign gets to declare — and Agamben later showed how that "temporary" suspension has become a permanent technique of governance. Film narrative is full of states of exception rendered visible: martial law declared and never lifted, checkpoints that outlast the crisis that created them, a curfew that becomes simply how things are now. What the concept exposes is the fiction of exception''s temporariness, the way emergency measures quietly calcify into the new normal on screen just as they do off it. The list below follows directors who film the exact seam where law suspends itself and never quite comes back.' where canon_slug = 'state-of-exception';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Arendt coined the phrase watching Eichmann testify, struck not by monstrous malice but by a bureaucrat''s flat, thoughtless adherence to procedure — evil that required no diabolical will, only the refusal to think about what one was doing. Cinema''s best villains in this register are unnervingly ordinary: the clerk stamping forms, the officer following orders with visible boredom, evil filmed not as spectacle but as paperwork, routine, a shrug. The chill isn''t in what such characters feel but in how little they seem to feel at all while doing terrible things efficiently. These readings trace performances built on that terrifying flatness rather than any theatrical villainy.' where canon_slug = 'banality-of-evil';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s death drive names a pull beneath the pleasure principle toward stasis, dissolution, and the compulsive repetition of painful scenarios the psyche can''t seem to stop replaying, as if repetition itself were more urgent than resolution. Films built around this logic loop their protagonists back into the same trauma, the same doomed choice, the same self-destructive gesture, refusing the tidy arc of growth in favor of a structure that circles rather than progresses. The unease it produces on screen is less about what happens than about the character''s evident will to make it happen again. The readings ahead follow that compulsive return across stories where moving forward was never really the point.' where canon_slug = 'death-drive-todestrieb-repetition-compulsion';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Althusser''s interpellation describes how ideology doesn''t just describe subjects but manufactures them, "hailing" an individual — turn around when the cop shouts "hey, you!" — into recognizing themselves as always already the subject the call presumed. Cinema restages this hailing constantly: a character answers to a role, a uniform, a name assigned by institution or family, and in the act of turning around becomes exactly what the system needed them to be. The unsettling power of the scene is that it looks like free response even though the subject position was waiting there in advance. Below, essays trace the moment on screen when characters turn around and recognize themselves in someone else''s call.' where canon_slug = 'interpellation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Hegel''s master-slave dialectic argues that domination is unstable at its root: the master needs the slave''s recognition to confirm his mastery, but recognition from someone reduced to a thing is worthless, while the slave''s labor on the world produces a self-consciousness the master never earns. Film plays this out in every story of a servant who quietly outgrows the one who commands them, every power reversal where the dependent party turns out to hold the more substantial claim to selfhood. What makes the structure durable on screen is its built-in irony — the position of command is revealed as parasitic on the very subordination it despises. These readings track that reversal across narratives where mastery slowly reveals its own poverty.' where canon_slug = 'master-slave-dialectic';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud distinguishes mourning, which eventually lets go of a lost object, from melancholia, which internalizes the loss so completely that the ego itself becomes the site of endless, unresolved self-reproach. Cinema draws the line vividly: a mourning character moves through ritual toward release, while a melancholic one seems to carry the dead inside them, speaking in their voice, punishing themselves for a loss they can''t metabolize into memory. The difference isn''t how sad someone looks but whether grief has an exit or has become identity. The following readings sit with characters who never quite finish grieving because the lost object has become who they are.' where canon_slug = 'melancholia-vs-mourning';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Nagel and Williams identify moral luck wherever factors outside an agent''s control end up determining how we judge them morally — the drunk driver who hits a pedestrian versus the equally drunk driver who gets home safe, judged worlds apart for an identical choice. Film is a natural laboratory for this discomfort, staging near-identical decisions that fork into wildly different outcomes by pure contingency, then daring the audience to notice they''re assigning blame based on results rather than intent. The unease comes from recognizing how much of ordinary moral judgment quietly depends on outcomes nobody actually chose. These essays follow scenes built to expose that fault line between what a character did and what merely happened to them.' where canon_slug = 'moral-luck';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Sublimation, in Freud''s account, is the process by which a drive that can''t be satisfied directly gets rerouted into a socially valued activity — the sexual or aggressive impulse transformed into art, ambition, or discipline without ever naming its origin. Films about obsessive creators often trace this exact conversion, showing a character''s painting, music, or violence-adjacent craft as the visible residue of an appetite the story never lets speak plainly. The fascination is watching a drive hide in plain sight, fully expressed yet fully deniable because it now wears the mask of achievement. The reading list below follows that rerouted energy from raw impulse to finished, presentable work.' where canon_slug = 'sublimation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s mirror stage describes the infant''s encounter with its own reflection — a moment of mistaken wholeness, where a fragmented, uncoordinated body identifies with an image that is more unified than the self actually is, founding the ego on a kind of productive misrecognition. Cinema, itself an art of reflections and doubles, restages this constantly: mirrors, screens, and surveillance monitors that hand a character an image of themselves they didn''t earn and can''t quite live up to. The tension the concept names is permanent, not developmental — the gap between the imaged self and the actual, uncoordinated one never fully closes. These readings track that gap wherever a film puts a character face to face with their own too-perfect reflection.' where canon_slug = 'the-mirror-stage';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kristeva''s abjection names what the self expels to become a self at all — the corpse, the wound, the curdled milk, anything that blurs the border between subject and object and provokes revulsion precisely because it was once inside. Horror cinema is abjection''s natural home, filming bodies that leak, transform, or refuse to stay properly bounded, forcing viewers to confront the fragile constructedness of their own clean edges. What makes abjection more than disgust is its intimacy — we recoil from the abject not because it''s alien but because it was never fully other to begin with. The list below follows films that use the body''s collapse to interrogate where selfhood actually begins.' where canon_slug = 'abjection';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Proust''s involuntary memory arrives uninvited — a taste, a smell, a texture that collapses decades in an instant, delivering a past no deliberate effort of recollection could summon on command. Film has its own version of the madeleine: a sound cue, an object glimpsed in passing, an unmotivated cut that drops a character (and the audience) into a past scene without warning or narrative permission. The power of the device lies in its involuntariness — a memory that arrives on its own terms carries a truth that willed remembering tends to edit away. These readings trace the moments cinema stages recall as ambush rather than search.' where canon_slug = 'involuntary-memory';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s Nachträglichkeit describes how an early experience only becomes traumatic retroactively, reorganized and given new meaning by a later event that the earlier scene couldn''t have understood on its own terms. Film narrative often structures itself this way, withholding the significance of an opening image until a later revelation reaches back and rewrites what we thought we''d already seen. The vertigo isn''t just plot twist — it''s the sense that meaning itself was never fixed at the moment of the original scene, only assigned after the fact. Below, essays follow films where the past keeps changing because the present hasn''t finished interpreting it.' where canon_slug = 'nachtraglichkeit';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Foucault''s panopticism describes a disciplinary architecture where the mere possibility of being watched — not constant actual surveillance — is enough to make subjects police themselves, internalizing the guard''s gaze until no guard is even needed. Films about institutions, prisons, workplaces, or surveilled cities often visualize this exact economy: a lone camera, an unmanned tower, a system that works because everyone behaves as if observed at all times. The concept''s real bite is that power stops needing to act once its subjects have started acting for it. These readings follow that internalized watchtower across stories where control has become self-administered.' where canon_slug = 'panopticism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Pateman''s sexual contract argues that the social contract''s story of free, equal individuals consenting to political order quietly depends on a prior, unmentioned contract of male sexual right over women — patriarchy isn''t outside modern liberal order but built into its founding fiction. Films that stage marriage, domestic arrangement, or civic belonging often reveal this buried clause, showing women''s consent as always already shaped by an unequal bargain the story pretends is mutual. The concept sharpens any scene where a woman''s choice looks free on its face but was negotiated inside terms she didn''t set. The list below follows films that quietly expose the contract beneath the contract.' where canon_slug = 'the-sexual-contract';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Where Baudrillard tracks the simulacrum''s final stage — the image with no original left to betray — Deleuze reads it as something more subversive, a copy that escapes the hierarchy of model and copy altogether and asserts its own difference. Cinema sits at the crossing of both readings: a film can either mourn the loss of the real behind endless reproduction, or celebrate the copy''s refusal to answer to any original at all. Which reading a given film invites often depends on tone — dread at a hollowed-out world versus delight at a world finally freed from the tyranny of the authentic. These readings track simulacra across films that can''t decide whether the copy is a loss or a liberation.' where canon_slug = 'simulacrum';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Set beside the more common contrast, this pairing asks a sharper question: not just how mourning and melancholia differ in outcome, but how a film''s very form — its editing rhythm, its willingness to let a scene end — can enact one or the other regardless of what characters say about their grief. A mourning film moves in sequence toward closure even if painfully; a melancholic one loops, returns, refuses temporal progression, formally repeating loss rather than narrating its passage. The distinction becomes structural rather than psychological, visible in pacing and repetition more than in dialogue about feelings. These essays read grief through film grammar itself rather than through what any character claims to feel.' where canon_slug = 'melancholia-versus-mourning';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Rancière''s distribution of the sensible names the prior partition — of what can be seen, said, and heard as meaningful — that determines who counts as a political subject before any argument even begins. Cinema, as a technology for deciding what enters the frame and what stays outside it, literalizes the concept: whose face gets a close-up, whose speech gets subtitled, whose suffering gets scored with music and whose passes in silence. Politics, in this account, isn''t what characters debate on screen but the prior editorial decision about who was allowed to appear as someone worth listening to. The readings below track that unspoken partition governing who gets to be sensible on screen at all.' where canon_slug = 'the-distribution-of-the-sensible';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Beyond the tower and the cell, Foucault''s disciplinary model extends into softer, dispersed apparatuses — schedules, exams, case files, the normalizing gaze of the clinic or the classroom — that produce docile bodies without ever needing a visible guard. Films tracking institutional life, hospitals, schools, workplaces, often dramatize this diffuse form of surveillance more than the literal panopticon, showing discipline as a thousand small evaluative gazes rather than one central watchtower. What makes this version unsettling is its invisibility — nobody has to be caught watching for the disciplining to still work. These essays trace surveillance dispersed so thoroughly through institutional life that no camera is even required.' where canon_slug = 'panopticism-disciplinary-surveillance';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lukács''s reification describes how, under capitalism, social relations between people come to appear as relations between things, so that labor, personality, even time itself gets experienced as an object with a market price rather than a lived process. Film visualizes this collapse sharply: a character reduced to a job title, a body treated as inventory, a life measured out in shifts and quotas until the human underneath becomes hard to locate. The unease isn''t exploitation alone but a deeper perceptual flattening, where people start looking at themselves the way the market already does. The readings ahead follow that thing-like gaze as it settles over characters who''ve started treating themselves as assets.' where canon_slug = 'reification';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Boym''s restorative nostalgia doesn''t just miss the past — it wants to rebuild it, literally, treating memory as a blueprint for a lost home or nation that can and should be reconstructed, truth and fabrication blurring in the process. Films about return, restoration projects, or nationalist longing often stage this same collapse, characters attempting to resurrect a house, a regime, or a childhood exactly as it "was," accumulating small distortions the story lets us see even when the character can''t. The danger the concept flags is nostalgia that stops admitting it''s nostalgia and starts insisting it''s simply accurate memory. The list below follows characters trying to rebuild a past that keeps quietly rewriting itself in the reconstruction.' where canon_slug = 'restorative-nostalgia';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Debord''s spectacle argues that in advanced capitalism, authentic social relation gets replaced by its representation — life is no longer lived directly but mediated through images, so thoroughly that the image relation becomes the primary social bond. Cinema is uniquely self-implicated here, an image-machine capable of critiquing spectacle while remaining one of its most efficient producers, staging characters who relate to their own lives as performances for an audience that may not even exist. The vertigo of the concept on screen is watching characters mistake being seen for actually living. These readings track films caught in that same bind, denouncing spectacle in the very medium that manufactures it.' where canon_slug = 'society-of-the-spectacle';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Levinas locates ethics not in rules but in the encounter with another person''s face, which issues a command — "thou shalt not kill" — that precedes any reasoning and obligates the self infinitely, before consent, before contract. Cinema''s close-up is arguably built for exactly this encounter, holding a face long enough that a viewer feels addressed rather than merely shown something, the ethical demand arriving through sheer proximity rather than plot. What the concept resists is any calculation of how much responsibility is owed — the face asks for everything, not a negotiated share. The readings collected here follow films that stage the close-up as an ethical summons rather than a formal device.' where canon_slug = 'the-face-of-the-other-and-infinite-responsibility';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'For Bazin, the photographic image is not a representation of reality but a kind of embalming of it, a trace produced without the intervention of human hands. Cinema inherits this ontological weight: a long take of a face or a landscape carries an indexical bond to what stood before the lens, which is why Bazin prized deep focus and duration over montage — cutting, for him, was a confession that the filmmaker didn''t trust the world to reveal itself. The question that keeps this concept alive is whether digital imaging, generated and manipulated pixel by pixel, still qualifies as an "image of" anything at all. The readings below trace that fault line from celluloid to CGI.' where canon_slug = 'the-ontology-of-the-photographic-image';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Debord''s spectacle names a social order in which lived experience has been replaced by its representation, where relations between people are mediated by images rather than direct exchange. Film is both diagnostic tool and prime offender here — a Hollywood blockbuster sells the spectator a passive relationship to their own desires, while certain art films turn the camera back on the apparatus that produces this alienation, exposing the seams. What makes the concept durable is that critique of the spectacle can itself become spectacular, absorbed and resold. The readings ahead follow that recursive trap through several decades of cinema.' where canon_slug = 'the-spectacle';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Austin''s performative utterance is speech that does something rather than merely describing it — "I now pronounce you," "I promise," words that constitute an act in the saying. Film dialogue is saturated with these moments: a vow, a verdict, a declaration of war spoken into a microphone, each one binding characters to consequences the instant the line is delivered. Directors who linger on such lines — holding on a face as a promise is made or broken — are staging the gap between saying and meaning that Austin spent his career mapping. The essays collected here follow that gap across genres.' where canon_slug = 'performative-utterance';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s screen memory is a vivid but innocuous recollection that stands in for, and conceals, a more disturbing one buried beneath it — memory as camouflage rather than record. Film narrative exploits exactly this structure whenever a character''s calm, sunlit childhood image turns out to be shielding trauma the plot only gradually admits, or when a director repeats an image obsessively without ever letting us see what it''s covering. The term also flatters cinema''s own vocabulary, since the "screen" doing the concealing is literalized on-screen. What follows are readings on films that dramatize memory as disguise.' where canon_slug = 'screen-memory';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s Name-of-the-Father is the symbolic function that installs law and prohibition into the subject, distinct from any actual father and often surviving his absence or failure. Cinema stages this obsessively through fathers who are dead, missing, or impotent yet whose authority still structures the narrative — a inheritance, a debt, a name on a door that governs the plot long after the man himself has exited it. The persistence of paternal law without a present patriarch is precisely what makes so many family melodramas and gangster films legible in Lacanian terms. The reading list below works through that persistence scene by scene.' where canon_slug = 'the-name-of-the-father';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'The sublime, for Kant and Burke, is the aesthetic response to what overwhelms comprehension — vastness, power, or terror that the mind cannot fully organize, producing a mix of pain and elevated pleasure. Cinema chases this feeling with storms, cosmic vistas, and scenes of catastrophe that dwarf the human figure on screen, letting spectators feel awe from the safety of a theater seat. The genre most explicit about this is disaster and space film, where the camera''s inability to contain its subject becomes the point rather than a technical failure. The essays ahead track how directors manufacture that overwhelm deliberately.' where canon_slug = 'the-sublime';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Levinas locates ethics not in abstract principle but in the encounter with the Other''s face, which issues a command — "thou shalt not kill" — prior to any reasoning or choice. Cinema''s close-up is arguably the only technology built to stage this encounter directly, filling the frame with a face that looks back, that resists being reduced to an object of the plot. Directors who hold on unbearable close-ups, refusing to cut away from suffering, are testing exactly how far a viewer''s responsibility extends once looking has begun. The readings collected here follow that test across very different traditions of filmmaking.' where canon_slug = 'responsibility-for-the-other-the-face';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Girard''s scapegoat mechanism describes how communities discharge internal violence by uniting against a single victim, a sacrifice that restores order precisely because its arbitrariness gets mistaken for guilt. Films that stage lynch mobs, witch hunts, or office scapegoating are reenacting this ancient machinery, and the more the narrative exposes the victim''s innocence, the more visible Girard''s mechanism becomes to the audience even as it remains invisible to the characters. Horror and Western genres return to this structure again and again, since both depend on a community''s need for someone to blame. The essays below trace the mechanism''s afterlife on screen.' where canon_slug = 'scapegoat-mechanism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Camus''s absurd names the confrontation between the human demand for meaning and a universe that offers none, a mismatch that cannot be resolved, only lived with. Cinema renders this less through argument than through texture — a character repeating a task with no destination, a plot that refuses closure, dialogue that circles without landing — creating the same vertigo Camus described in Sisyphus''s endless labor. The absurd resists being filmed directly, which is why the films that capture it best tend to work by tone and rhythm rather than statement. The readings ahead sample that range of approaches.' where canon_slug = 'the-absurd';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'This companion strand of Levinas''s thought pushes the ethical demand of the face further: responsibility for the Other is infinite, exceeding any contract, reciprocity, or calculation the subject might attempt. Where cinema takes this seriously, it tends to abandon narrative economy altogether, letting a face occupy the frame past the point where the story needs it to, forcing an ethical duration onto the viewer rather than an informational one. The wager across these films is that infinite responsibility can only be gestured at through excess — too much time, too much stillness. The pieces below examine that excess as a formal strategy.' where canon_slug = 'the-face-and-infinite-responsibility-for-the-other';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bourdieu''s cultural capital names the non-economic assets — taste, education, manner, accent — that function as currency within a given social field, convertible into status and, eventually, into money. Film is obsessed with staging these conversions: the outsider who learns which fork to use, the parvenu whose wealth can''t purchase the right vocabulary, the arthouse snob who trades obscure references for social standing. What makes the concept sharp on screen is how visibly cinema can mark the gap between having capital and merely performing it. The readings that follow track that performance across class-conscious cinema.' where canon_slug = 'cultural-capital';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Sartre''s formula existence precedes essence holds that a person has no predetermined nature; we exist first, then define ourselves through choices, which makes us radically responsible for who we become. Cinematic protagonists who reject an assigned role — a soldier who won''t fight, a wife who walks out with no plan — dramatize this freedom as vertigo rather than liberation, since the absence of a script is precisely what terrifies them. The genre most committed to this premise is the existentialist drama, where plot often stalls specifically so the character''s freedom has room to appear. The essays ahead follow that stalling as a deliberate device.' where canon_slug = 'existence-precedes-essence';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Fetishistic disavowal is the structure of "I know very well, but nevertheless" — a subject who consciously registers an unwelcome truth while continuing to act as though it weren''t so, a formula running from Freud through Mannoni to Žižek''s account of ideology. Cinema audiences perform this constantly: knowing a screen story is fabricated while still weeping at it, or knowing a political catastrophe is real while consuming its spectacle as entertainment. Films that stage characters clinging to comforting fictions despite full awareness are essentially filming this disavowal from the outside. The readings below examine how that gap between knowledge and belief gets visualized.' where canon_slug = 'fetishistic-disavowal';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Butler''s gender performativity argues that gender is not an expression of some inner truth but an effect produced by repeated, stylized acts — there is no doer behind the deed, only the doing. Cinema is a natural laboratory for this claim, since actors are already performing gender as craft, and films about drag, impersonation, or characters caught mid-transformation expose the citational repetition that makes gender legible as gender in the first place. The more a film insists on gender as innate, the more its own performances betray Butler''s point. The essays ahead trace performativity through several kinds of cinematic disguise.' where canon_slug = 'gender-performativity';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Performativity, joining Austin''s speech-act theory to Butler''s account of identity, treats certain acts of saying or doing as constitutive rather than descriptive — they bring into being the very thing they name. Cinema multiplies this logic beyond gender: a character becomes a criminal by being called one on camera, a nation gets narrated into existence through newsreel footage, an actor''s repeated gestures harden into a persona indistinguishable from a self. The unifying thread is that identity in these films is never discovered, only enacted, over and over, until it sticks. The readings below follow that logic across several disciplines it has colonized.' where canon_slug = 'performativity';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Klein''s projective identification describes a defense in which unbearable feelings are expelled into another person, who is then unconsciously pressured to embody them — projection with a hook that pulls the object in. Films built around toxic couples or claustrophobic family units often stage this with uncanny precision, one character''s rage or fear seeping into another until it''s unclear whose feeling is whose, the camera itself sometimes mimicking that confusion through disorienting point-of-view shifts. Psychological thrillers depend on this mechanism more than they usually admit. The pieces ahead unpack how that expulsion and absorption gets blocked and shot.' where canon_slug = 'projective-identification';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Mulvey''s male gaze names the way classical Hollywood cinema organizes looking around a masculine, heterosexual viewing position, with the camera, the male characters, and the audience all aligned to consume the female body as spectacle. Scopophilia, the pleasure in looking itself, is what fuels this apparatus — close-ups that fragment a woman''s body, narrative pauses that stall plot for display, editing that stitches the spectator''s eye to the protagonist''s. Decades of filmmaking since have either reproduced this structure unreflectively or actively dismantled it, which is what keeps the argument contested rather than settled. The readings below sample both sides of that fight.' where canon_slug = 'the-male-gaze-scopophilia';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'McLuhan''s claim that the medium is the message insists that a technology''s form reshapes perception and social life more decisively than any content it carries — television changes us regardless of what''s on it. Cinema, as both subject and object of this idea, offers constant test cases: a film about television becomes an argument about television''s effects simply by being watched on a screen, and directors who foreground format — aspect ratio, grain, aspect of exhibition — are making McLuhan''s point through form rather than dialogue. The concept ages strangely well as each new screen technology arrives to prove it again. The essays ahead trace that recurrence.' where canon_slug = 'the-medium-is-the-message';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Derrida''s archive fever describes the contradictory drive to preserve the past compulsively while that very compulsion works to destroy or distort it, since no archive is a neutral repository — it''s shaped by exclusion, technology, and the death drive lurking beneath the desire to remember. Found-footage films and documentaries built from damaged or incomplete archives dramatize this directly, their gaps and artifacts becoming the actual subject rather than an obstacle to it. The fever intensifies with digital media, where infinite storage promises total memory even as formats and platforms constantly go extinct. The readings below follow archives that keep failing at their own purpose.' where canon_slug = 'archive-fever';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Foucault''s biopolitics names a mode of power that manages populations as biological entities — birth rates, health, hygiene, life expectancy — rather than ruling through the sovereign''s right to kill. Films set in hospitals, quarantine zones, or bureaucratic health systems stage this administrative gaze literally, with characters reduced to statistics, case numbers, or risk categories the moment institutional cameras or paperwork enter the frame. Pandemic and dystopian cinema in particular has become a genre laboratory for imagining biopolitics pushed to its logical extreme. The essays ahead examine how that administrative logic gets visualized.' where canon_slug = 'biopolitics';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Biopower, Foucault''s companion term, is the productive counterpart to biopolitics — the mechanisms, from medicine to demography to discipline, through which power actively fosters, optimizes, and regulates life rather than merely threatening death. Cinema that follows institutions calibrating bodies — fitness regimes, prison systems, reproductive technologies — is charting biopower''s machinery in action, often through repetitive procedural detail that mimics the very normalization it depicts. What separates this from its sibling concept is the emphasis on power that makes live rather than lets die. The readings below trace that productive, managing power through several institutional settings.' where canon_slug = 'biopower';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Fisher''s capitalist realism describes the pervasive sense that capitalism is not just the dominant system but the only imaginable one, foreclosing alternatives so thoroughly that even the end of the world feels easier to picture than the end of capitalism. Films saturated with corporate logos, gig work, and dead-end futures without utopian horizon are staging this foreclosure, often through a flatness of tone that refuses the drama of open rebellion because rebellion itself has been pre-absorbed. The mood these films produce is less outrage than exhaustion, which is exactly Fisher''s point. The essays ahead follow that exhausted mood through contemporary cinema.' where canon_slug = 'capitalist-realism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Berlant''s cruel optimism names an attachment to a fantasy of the good life — upward mobility, romantic fulfillment, stable work — that actively obstructs the flourishing it promises, yet remains impossible to give up. Films about characters clinging to a dream job, a failing relationship, or an American promise long past its expiration date are staging this bind precisely, with the tragedy located not in losing the object but in refusing to release it. The genre payoff is a kind of slow-motion devastation rather than a single climactic loss. The readings below track that devastation as it accumulates.' where canon_slug = 'cruel-optimism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bataille''s dépense, or unproductive expenditure, values loss, waste, and excess for their own sake against an economy organized entirely around accumulation and utility — sacrifice, festival, and orgiastic spending as forms of sovereign freedom. Cinema of excess, whether in extravagant musical numbers, apocalyptic destruction, or characters who burn through fortunes and bodies without any productive return, is performing dépense on the level of spectacle itself, refusing narrative economy along with economic economy. The pleasure such films offer is the pleasure of waste, unapologetically. The essays ahead follow that refusal of usefulness across several traditions.' where canon_slug = 'depense';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Hauntology, coined by Derrida and adapted by Fisher for cultural criticism, describes how the present is haunted by lost futures — the abandoned promises of past eras that linger as spectral static rather than fully departing. Films built from degraded VHS textures, analog synth scores, or plots about futures that never arrived are working in this register, mourning not a person but an entire cancelled timeline. The nostalgia here is not for what was, but for what was supposed to come and didn''t. The readings below trace that spectral mourning through recent cinema.' where canon_slug = 'hauntology';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Mono no aware, a concept from Japanese aesthetics, names the bittersweet awareness of impermanence — the poignancy that arises precisely because things, and people, do not last. Cinema captures this through images of transience made deliberately central rather than incidental: falling petals, a fading season, a quiet domestic moment the film knows will not recur, all filmed with an acceptance that refuses melodrama. Directors working in this register let sadness arrive without amplifying it, trusting the mere passage of time to do the emotional work. The essays ahead follow that trust across several films built on fleetingness.' where canon_slug = 'mono-no-aware';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Nietzsche''s ressentiment describes a corrosive psychology in which the powerless, unable to act directly against those who dominate them, invert their values instead, recasting weakness as virtue and strength as vice. Cinematic villains and antiheroes driven by resentment rather than desire dramatize this inversion vividly, their moral language always arriving a beat after the injury that actually motivates them. Revenge narratives are the genre most structurally dependent on ressentiment, since they require audiences to feel the justice of an inverted value system for the duration of the film. The readings below track that inversion as it plays out on screen.' where canon_slug = 'ressentiment';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'The struggle for recognition, running from Hegel''s master-slave dialectic through Honneth''s contemporary elaboration, holds that selfhood is not self-generated but depends on being acknowledged by another consciousness, making recognition a site of both conflict and mutual dependency. Films built around a character demanding to be seen — by a parent, an institution, a society that refuses to look — are staging this dialectic almost too literally, with humiliation and confrontation functioning as the visible symptoms of recognition withheld. The genre spans court dramas to family melodramas because the structure fits both equally well. The essays ahead sample that range.' where canon_slug = 'struggle-for-recognition';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kant''s mathematical sublime is distinct from its dynamical counterpart in being provoked by sheer magnitude rather than force — a scale so vast the imagination fails to comprehend it in a single intuition, producing a strange pleasure in that very failure. Cinema stages this through images of overwhelming scale that resist a single vantage point: cosmic distances, crowd shots stretching past the frame''s edge, architecture the camera cannot contain no matter how it tracks. The mathematical sublime is less about danger than about arithmetic defeat, the mind quite literally running out of room to count. The readings below follow that defeat across several visual traditions.' where canon_slug = 'the-mathematical-sublime';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'The work of mourning, theorized by Freud and later reworked by Derrida, describes the psychic labor required to withdraw attachment from a lost object, a process that Derrida complicates by suggesting full completion may be neither possible nor even ethically desirable. Films that dwell in grief''s aftermath — rituals repeated past their usefulness, a character speaking to the dead, an object kept charged with meaning long after it should have faded — are staging this labor as ongoing rather than resolved. The tension between finishing mourning and betraying the dead by finishing it is what gives these films their peculiar unease. The essays ahead follow that unease to its different resolutions.' where canon_slug = 'the-work-of-mourning';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'A heterotopia, in Foucault''s formulation, is a real place that functions as a counter-site, simultaneously representing and inverting the ordinary spaces of a culture. Cinema keeps returning to these enclosed, rule-bound zones—ships, asylums, brothels, prisons, hotels—where normal social logic is suspended and a different, often unsettling order takes over; the camera lingers on thresholds, corridors, and locked doors as if mapping the boundary between one world and its shadow. The eleven readings below trace how filmmakers turn architecture itself into an argument about power and deviation.' where canon_slug = 'heterotopia';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Ferenczi described a defense in which a person facing overwhelming threat unconsciously absorbs the aggressor''s will, mistaking submission for a kind of survival. Films dramatize this collapse through hostages who begin echoing their captors'' logic, children who mimic an abusive parent''s cruelty toward others, or soldiers who adopt the very violence done to them, often signaled by a shift in the victim''s posture, vocabulary, or gaze. Eleven readings unpack how the concept complicates easy divisions between perpetrator and victim on screen.' where canon_slug = 'identification-with-the-aggressor';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Klein''s manic defense names the frantic swing into triumph, control, and contempt that shields the psyche from the depressive anxieties of loss and guilt. On screen it shows up as forced gaiety after catastrophe, hyperactive scheming that outruns grief, or a character''s compulsive need to dominate a situation rather than mourn it—think of the party thrown the night after a death, or the joke told too quickly after violence. These eleven readings follow that manic pitch across genres where cheerfulness itself becomes suspect.' where canon_slug = 'manic-defense';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Mise en abyme, borrowed from Gide and heraldry, describes a work that contains a miniature reflection of itself nested within its own structure. Film achieves this through plays-within-films, characters watching their own story unfold on a screen, or a shot composition that recursively frames another frame, producing a dizzying sense that the work is always commenting on its own making. The eleven pieces gathered here follow that mirrored spiral wherever directors use it to fold a film back on itself.' where canon_slug = 'mise-en-abyme';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s repetition compulsion describes the drive to reenact painful or traumatic experiences, not despite their unpleasure but because of a deeper pull he called the death drive, a tendency toward stasis and dissolution beneath the pleasure principle. Cinema stages this through characters trapped in loops—returning to the scene of a crime, replaying a doomed relationship, or a narrative structure that literally repeats scenes with variation—turning formal repetition into psychic symptom. Eleven readings track how films use recurrence to visualize what the psyche cannot simply remember or forget.' where canon_slug = 'repetition-compulsion-death-drive';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'For Levinas, encountering the face of the Other places an infinite ethical demand on the self prior to any contract, choice, or reciprocity. Film renders this confrontation through close-ups that refuse to let a face become mere image, or through plots where a protagonist is interrupted by a stranger''s vulnerability and cannot look away without becoming complicit. The eleven readings collected here examine how directors use framing and duration to make the viewer feel that same unchosen obligation.' where canon_slug = 'responsibility-for-the-other';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Agamben''s state of exception describes the moment sovereign power suspends the law in the name of preserving it, producing bare life—existence stripped of political standing and exposed to total control. Films set in camps, border zones, and martial-law cities visualize this through checkpoints, curfews, and bodies reduced to their mere biological survival, often via stark lighting or clinical framing that empties out individual identity. These eleven readings follow that logic of suspended law across very different cinematic worlds.' where canon_slug = 'state-of-exception-bare-life';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Chion''s acousmêtre is a voice heard without its source being seen, a disembodied presence that seems to know, see, or control everything precisely because it cannot be located. Horror and thriller cinema exploit this through unseen narrators, phone callers, or looming authority figures—directors withhold the reveal for as long as possible, using off-screen space, distorted telephony, and public-address systems to let the voice roam freely through the frame, before a single shot of the speaking mouth collapses that power back into an ordinary, vulnerable body. Eleven readings trace how sound design manufactures this uncanny omnipotence and then dismantles it.' where canon_slug = 'the-acousmetre';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s mirror stage describes the infant''s formative misrecognition, or méconnaissance, of a fragmented self in the coherent, idealized image reflected back to it. Cinema restages this primal scene constantly, through literal mirrors, doubles, and screens within screens where characters mistake an image for wholeness, catching themselves in a reflection that looks more composed and masterful than they actually feel—and through the spectator''s own absorption in the fantasy of a unified, controlling gaze, seduced by an on-screen ideal-ego the way an infant is seduced by its own reflection. The eleven readings here follow that gap between the imagined self and the fractured one underneath.' where canon_slug = 'the-mirror-stage-meconnaissance';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kierkegaard''s teleological suspension of the ethical describes the moment faith demands a leap beyond universal moral law, exemplified by Abraham''s willingness to sacrifice Isaac. Films built around absolute commitments—religious, familial, or ideological—stage this same terrifying suspension, asking audiences to watch a character abandon shared ethical ground for a private, incommunicable conviction, often by withholding the interior justification entirely so the act arrives as pure, unexplained rupture rather than persuasive argument. Eleven readings examine how directors frame that leap as either sublime or monstrous, often refusing to resolve the difference.' where canon_slug = 'the-teleological-suspension-of-the-ethical';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Winnicott''s transitional object is the blanket, toy, or habit that helps a child bridge the gap between total dependence and a separately existing world, holding both fantasy and reality at once. Films track adults who cling to comparable objects—photographs, letters, recorded voices—that let them negotiate loss, separation, or the fear of an indifferent reality, often filming these objects in unusually intimate close-up. The eleven readings gathered here follow that clinging as it moves from nursery to full adult grief.' where canon_slug = 'transitional-object';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Benjamin warned that fascism resolves political contradiction not by addressing it but by aestheticizing it, turning mass spectacle and self-destruction into beauty. Cinema is uniquely implicated here, since the very techniques that make political rallies, uniforms, and mass choreography photogenic—symmetrical crowd formations, low-angle heroics, triumphant score—can seduce an audience into admiring what it should resist, whether the film intends critique or propaganda, and later filmmakers must find ways to quote that grammar of spectacle without simply reproducing its pleasure. Ten readings trace this dangerous overlap between cinematic form and political seduction.' where canon_slug = 'aestheticization-of-politics';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Sartre''s mauvaise foi, or bad faith, names the self-deception by which a person denies their own freedom, pretending to be a fixed thing rather than a set of open choices. Film characters perform this constantly—hiding behind roles, routines, or excuses of circumstance to avoid the vertigo of their own agency—and directors often expose the lie through a single unguarded gesture that breaks the performance. Ten readings follow how cinema catches people in the act of fleeing their own freedom.' where canon_slug = 'mauvaise-foi';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Abraham and Torok''s melancholic incorporation describes how an unspoken loss gets swallowed whole and sealed inside the psyche as a secret crypt, rather than being mourned and metabolized through the slower work of grief. Films dramatize this through characters who inherit a family silence, repeat a dead relative''s gestures without knowing why, or carry an object—a room left untouched, a name never spoken—that functions as a tomb for what was never said aloud, with the camera often returning obsessively to that sealed space. Ten readings excavate how cinema visualizes these buried, unspeakable losses.' where canon_slug = 'melancholic-incorporation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Fricker''s testimonial injustice occurs when a speaker''s credibility is unfairly discounted because of prejudice against their social identity, silencing them before their words are even weighed on their merits. Courtroom dramas, journalism films, and stories of marginalized witnesses dramatize this through scenes where a character''s account is visibly dismissed, interrupted, or reframed by someone with more institutional authority, and directors can either reproduce that dismissal through editing that favors the skeptic''s point of view or expose it by lingering on the speaker''s face until disbelief starts to look like the real injustice. Ten readings track how film form itself can either replicate or expose this unequal distribution of belief.' where canon_slug = 'testimonial-injustice';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s big Other is the symbolic order itself, the imagined authority or social structure whose gaze and judgment organize desire even when no single person embodies it. Film gives this abstraction a face through surveillance systems, unseen judges, or vague social consensus that characters obey without ever confronting directly, often visualized as an empty chair, a distant office, or a camera no one controls. Ten readings follow that invisible authority as it shapes cinematic desire and guilt alike.' where canon_slug = 'the-big-other';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kant''s dynamical sublime describes the mixture of terror and exhilaration felt before an overwhelming natural force viewed from a position of safety, where reason ultimately triumphs over sensory overwhelm. Disaster films, war epics, and nature documentaries chase this effect through storms, avalanches, and battle sequences shot to dwarf the human figure while still framing the viewer as safely outside the danger. Ten readings examine how cinema manufactures that safe distance necessary for awe rather than mere fear.' where canon_slug = 'the-dynamical-sublime';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Turner''s frontier thesis argued that the ever-receding line between civilization and wilderness forged a distinctly American character defined by individualism and self-reinvention. Westerns and their revisions dramatize this through wagon trains, fences, and horizons that keep moving just out of reach, framing the lone rider against vast open land as the visual embodiment of that mythic self-making, while later films expose the thesis''s blind spots by showing whose land and history that frontier erased, turning the same landscape into a site of dispossession rather than opportunity. Ten readings track this myth from its heroic version to its critical unraveling on screen.' where canon_slug = 'the-frontier-thesis';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Merleau-Ponty''s lived body rejects the split between mind and mechanical flesh, insisting that perception happens through a body already entangled with, and oriented by, the world it perceives. Films that dwell on physical sensation—texture, effort, injury, the disorientation of illness or disability—use close, embodied camerawork, shifting focal depth, and sound mixed to mimic strained breath or fatigue, making viewers feel perception as inseparable from motion and touch rather than detached observation from a fixed vantage point. Ten readings follow how cinema tries to film thinking as something the body does.' where canon_slug = 'the-lived-body';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Standing''s precariat names an emerging class defined not by poverty alone but by chronic insecurity, unstable labor, and the absence of any predictable future to plan around. Contemporary films about gig work, temporary contracts, and eviction dramatize this through fragmented schedules, transient housing, phones that ping with new demands at all hours, and bodies visibly worn down by uncertainty rather than by a single dramatic catastrophe, favoring accumulation over incident as the true engine of the drama. Ten readings trace how cinema visualizes a life lived permanently on the edge of falling.' where canon_slug = 'the-precariat';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Rooted in Hegel and developed by Honneth, the struggle for recognition holds that selfhood only forms through being acknowledged by another consciousness, making misrecognition a genuine social wound rather than a mere insult. Films about outsiders demanding to be seen—by family, institutions, or a hostile public—stage this struggle through confrontations where a character insists on their own reality against a world determined to ignore or diminish it. Ten readings follow that demand for acknowledgment across strikingly different social settings.' where canon_slug = 'the-struggle-for-recognition';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Nietzsche''s amor fati calls for loving one''s fate entirely, affirming even suffering and loss as necessary rather than merely enduring or resigning oneself to them. Films that end not in triumph but in a character''s full embrace of what has happened to them—illness, ruin, mortality—stage this affirmation through a final calm that refuses tragedy''s usual despair, often marked by a change in pacing, light, or music that lets the ending feel like acceptance rather than defeat. Nine readings explore how cinema tries to make that difficult love visible rather than merely stated.' where canon_slug = 'amor-fati';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Gramsci''s cultural hegemony describes how ruling classes secure consent not through force alone but by making their worldview seem like simple, unquestionable common sense, absorbed rather than imposed. Film both transmits and interrogates this process, whether through genre conventions that naturalize a particular social order—the happy ending that rewards conformity, the villain whose defeat restores an unexamined status quo—or through works that expose how thoroughly ordinary language, taste, and aspiration have been shaped by power. Nine readings track hegemony as it operates in and through the moving image itself.' where canon_slug = 'cultural-hegemony';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Deleuze and Guattari''s deterritorialization names the process by which flows of desire, capital, or identity break free from fixed structures and coded hierarchies, only to be recaptured and reterritorialized elsewhere in a new, often more rigid form. Films that follow migrants, nomadic characters, or unstable spatial arrangements—borders that dissolve, maps that no longer apply, cities that reassemble themselves mid-story—use disjointed editing, discontinuous geography, and refusal of a stable establishing shot to enact this constant escape and recapture rather than simply narrating it. Nine readings trace how cinema can move like the flows this theory describes.' where canon_slug = 'deterritorialization';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bourdieu''s distinction and cultural capital describe how taste itself, seemingly a personal preference formed in private, functions as a coded marker of class position, education, and inherited privilege passed down like property. Films about art worlds, universities, or social climbing stage this through scenes of subtle judgment—the wrong wine, the wrong reference, the wrong laugh at the wrong moment—where cultural fluency becomes a currency more exclusionary than money itself, and outsiders are caught performing a fluency they never quite acquired. Nine readings follow how cinema exposes the quiet violence of good taste.' where canon_slug = 'distinction-cultural-capital';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Sedgwick''s epistemology of the closet argues that the modern distinction between the secret and the known has been organized around the structuring possibility of homosexual disclosure, shaping far more than sexuality alone. Films built around concealment and coming out use visual withholding—averted eyes, locked rooms, information the camera keeps but the audience senses—to dramatize how a secret''s mere possibility reorganizes every relationship around it. Nine readings trace that logic of the closet across very different narrative disguises.' where canon_slug = 'epistemology-of-the-closet';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Berkeley''s esse est percipi, to be is to be perceived, proposes that existence itself depends on being observed by some mind. Cinema, an art built entirely on the image offered to a perceiving eye, seems almost designed to test this claim, particularly in films obsessed with surveillance, performance for unseen audiences, or characters who seem to flicker out of reality once no one is watching them. Nine readings follow how film turns a philosophical puzzle into a genuinely unsettling formal device.' where canon_slug = 'esse-est-percipi';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Anderson''s imagined community describes the nation as a construct sustained by shared media and narrative rather than face-to-face contact, allowing millions of strangers who will never meet to feel bound together by a common story and calendar. Films that circulate national mythologies—through anthems, newsreels, or synchronized broadcasts watched simultaneously across a country—dramatize how cinema itself, projected in identical form to scattered audiences, has served as one of the primary machines for manufacturing that felt bond. Nine readings trace how movies build, and sometimes fracture, this imagined solidarity.' where canon_slug = 'imagined-community';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Levinas pushes ethical obligation past any calculable limit, arguing that responsibility for the Other is infinite, exceeding what reciprocity or justice could ever demand back. Films that follow characters undone by an obligation they cannot refuse or complete—caring for someone beyond exhaustion, bearing witness beyond what testimony can hold—use accumulating duration and refusal of resolution to make that infinity felt rather than argued. Nine readings track how cinema stretches ethical demand past any comfortable closure.' where canon_slug = 'infinite-responsibility-for-the-other';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lacan''s jouissance names a pleasure that exceeds the pleasure principle, shading into pain, transgression, and a satisfaction the subject cannot fully want or disown. Films that linger on excess—violence enjoyed too much, desire that ruins the desirer, an ending that feels wrong to feel good about—stage this uncomfortable surplus through tonal instability that keeps viewers from settling into simple approval or disgust. Nine readings follow jouissance as it destabilizes the moral clarity cinema usually promises.' where canon_slug = 'jouissance';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'In Lacanian psychoanalysis, the Name-of-the-Father names the paternal function that inserts the child into language and law, breaking the imaginary fusion with the mother and installing the structure of desire itself. Cinema stages this rupture obsessively: absent fathers whose authority still governs the plot, patriarchs whose literal death triggers a symbolic crisis, or surrogate figures — judges, priests, mob bosses — who occupy the position without occupying the role. When a film''s antagonist turns out to be less a person than a rule, this concept is usually the reason. The reading list below traces how directors dramatize the difference between a father and the Father.' where canon_slug = 'name-of-the-father';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Recuperation, as the Situationists used the term, describes how radical gestures get absorbed back into the market they once opposed, sold as style once their threat is neutralized. Films that stage rebellion often perform this fate in real time — punk aesthetics repackaged as merchandise within the narrative, revolutionary slogans printed on the very products they condemned, a countercultural hero''s image licensed after their death. The irony is structural, not accidental: the more visible the critique, the more valuable it becomes as content. These readings follow the concept from Debord''s spectacle theory into cinema''s own complicity in selling resistance.' where canon_slug = 'recuperation';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'This concept, sharpened in debates following Sontag, asks what happens when pain becomes visually composed — when suffering is lit, framed, and paced for beauty rather than simply witnessed. Cinema faces this tension constantly: a slow-motion massacre, a tastefully shot famine, a torture scene scored with strings, all of which risk converting horror into pleasure for the viewer. The question isn''t whether such images are permissible but whether their craft quietly launders the violence they depict. The list ahead examines films that either fall into this trap or interrogate it directly.' where canon_slug = 'the-aestheticization-of-suffering';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kant''s categorical imperative demands that persons be treated as ends in themselves, never merely as means to someone else''s project, however noble that project claims to be. Films built around utilitarian dilemmas — sacrifice one to save many, deceive someone for their own good, use a person''s body or labor as an instrument — stage exactly the violation this principle forbids, and audiences usually feel the wrongness before they can name it. The tension is sharpest when a sympathetic character does the instrumentalizing. What follows are readings that use Kantian ethics to unpack cinema''s most uncomfortable moral math.' where canon_slug = 'the-categorical-imperative-treating-persons-as-ends';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Coined by Lefebvre and later expanded by Harvey, the right to the city insists that urban space belongs to those who inhabit and produce it, not merely to capital that develops and profits from it. Films about gentrification, eviction, and redevelopment dramatize this claim in concrete terms — a neighborhood rezoned overnight, a community displaced for a stadium, a skyline that rises as its former residents are priced out of frame. The camera itself becomes a stakeholder, choosing whose city gets shown. These readings track how urban cinema turns planning documents into human drama.' where canon_slug = 'the-right-to-the-city';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Michel Chion''s acousmêtre names a voice whose source we hear but cannot see, a sound unanchored from a visible body that consequently gains an eerie, almost omniscient power. Horror and thriller cinema exploit this constantly — the villain heard before seen, the disembodied narrator, the intercom voice that seems to know more than any embodied character could. The moment that voice finally gets a face, much of its menace typically drains away, which tells you where the power actually lived. This list follows Chion''s theory through cinema''s long fascination with sound before sight.' where canon_slug = 'acousmatic-sound-the-acousmetre';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bardo, from Tibetan Buddhist thought, designates the liminal interval between death and rebirth, a suspended state where identity is unmoored and consequences of a life are processed before what comes next. Films that trap characters in loops, waystations, or ambiguous afterlives borrow this structure whether or not they name it — think of protagonists wandering spaces that feel neither alive nor fully gone, replaying memory as a kind of purification. The device lets cinema visualize a psychological threshold as literal geography. The readings collected here explore how this in-between state becomes cinematic form.' where canon_slug = 'bardo';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'At its core, the categorical imperative asks whether the principle behind an action could be willed as a universal law without contradiction — a test of consistency rather than consequence. Cinema loves to place characters at exactly this fork: the lie that only works if others don''t lie, the exception someone grants themselves but no one else. Watching a protagonist justify a singular act as somehow not setting a precedent is often the clearest way a film smuggles in Kantian critique without a lecture. The list below gathers films that turn this abstract test into dramatic stakes.' where canon_slug = 'categorical-imperative';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Marx''s commodity fetishism describes how objects on the market appear to possess value intrinsically, obscuring the human labor that actually produced them and the social relations behind that labor. Films set in factories, sweatshops, or luxury retail often peel back this veil deliberately, cutting from a glossy product shot to the hands that made it, or tracking a single object''s journey to expose the invisible chain behind its price tag. The gap between exchange value and the labor it conceals becomes, in these films, the whole plot. These readings follow that concept from Marx''s economics into cinematic exposure.' where canon_slug = 'commodity-fetishism-exchange-value';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bourdieu''s cultural capital argues that taste itself functions as currency, sorting people by class through their command of language, art, and etiquette long before money changes hands. Film narratives about class mobility depend on this constantly — the outsider who must learn which fork to use, the parvenu whose wealth can''t disguise an accent, the old-money family that polices boundaries through references rather than rules. What makes someone belong is rarely stated outright; it''s performed. This reading list traces how cinema stages distinction as a silent, brutal gatekeeping mechanism.' where canon_slug = 'cultural-capital-distinction';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Foucault''s disciplinary power operates not through spectacular violence but through constant surveillance and normalization, producing docile bodies that regulate themselves before any guard needs to intervene. Prison films, school dramas, and military pictures are natural laboratories for this idea — the panoptic tower, the roll call, the uniform that disciplines posture as much as it clothes the body. The genius of the concept is that it explains control without a single visible whip. The essays ahead track how cinema visualizes power that works by shaping conduct rather than punishing it.' where canon_slug = 'disciplinary-power-docile-bodies';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Hochschild''s emotional labor names the work of managing one''s own feelings to produce a publicly required emotional display, most visible in service jobs where a smile is part of the wage. Films set in call centers, flight cabins, or hospitality often dramatize the toll of this performance — the plastered grin that costs more than physical labor, the private breakdown that follows a shift of relentless cheerfulness. The camera can catch what customers never see: the seam between felt emotion and performed emotion. This list follows that seam through cinema''s workplace dramas.' where canon_slug = 'emotional-labor';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Althusser''s Ideological State Apparatus describes how institutions like schools, churches, and media reproduce a society''s dominant ideology not through force but through everyday practice, interpellating individuals as subjects who consent to their own positions. Films set inside classrooms, newsrooms, or churches frequently expose this machinery mid-operation, showing how a lesson, sermon, or broadcast quietly manufactures belief while appearing neutral. The most chilling scenes are often the most mundane ones. These readings examine how cinema catches ideology in the act of reproducing itself.' where canon_slug = 'ideological-state-apparatus';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Born in the pages of Cahiers du Cinéma and championed by Truffaut, la politique des auteurs insisted that even within industrial studio production, a director''s signature could unify a body of work into something like personal expression. The theory reshaped how films get read, treating recurring obsessions, camera habits, and thematic fingerprints as evidence of authorship equal to any novelist''s. It also sparked lasting arguments about collaboration, credit, and whether "vision" can survive a hundred crew members. The list below traces how this polemic became film criticism''s founding argument.' where canon_slug = 'la-politique-des-auteurs';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Ricoeur''s narrative identity proposes that selfhood is constituted through the stories we tell about our lives, stitching disconnected events into a coherent plot with a beginning, middle, and implied meaning. Films about memory loss, unreliable narration, or life review literalize this claim, showing characters who must actively author themselves to remain intelligible even to their own minds. When a film''s structure mirrors its protagonist''s fractured self-narrative, this concept is doing the heavy lifting. These readings explore how cinema treats identity as something told rather than simply had.' where canon_slug = 'narrative-identity';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Kierkegaard''s repetition is not mere recurrence but a movement forward that reclaims the past by living it again with new inwardness — repetition as a form of faith rather than stagnation. Films built on loops, rituals, or returns to the same place test exactly this distinction: does going back change anything, or trap the character further? The difference between a film that ends in despair and one that ends in redemption often hinges on whether repetition becomes revelation. The essays ahead follow this philosophical wrinkle through cinema''s many circular structures.' where canon_slug = 'repetition';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s account of repression describes how unacceptable desires or memories get pushed out of consciousness, only to resurface distorted — as symptom, slip, or symbol — because repression never actually destroys what it buries. Horror and family melodrama are especially fluent in this logic: the house with a secret room, the trauma that erupts as violence generations later, the calm surface cracked by something that refuses to stay hidden. The genre conventions of the uncanny are, in a sense, Freud''s theory wearing a mask. This list tracks that return across cinema''s haunted structures.' where canon_slug = 'repression-and-the-return-of-the-repressed';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Zuboff''s surveillance capitalism describes an economic order that extracts human experience as raw data, converting behavior into predictive products sold back to advertisers and institutions. Contemporary films about tech companies, smart devices, and algorithmic manipulation increasingly stage this extraction directly, turning the omniscient screen or app into a silent antagonist that profits from watching. What makes these films unsettling is how mundane the surveillance looks — no dystopian uniforms, just terms of service nobody read. The readings below examine how cinema visualizes an economy built on watching.' where canon_slug = 'surveillance-capitalism';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Bourdieu''s symbolic violence names domination so naturalized that both the powerful and the dominated accept it as the way things simply are, requiring no physical force to reproduce inequality. Films about class, gender, or colonial hierarchy often show this violence operating through etiquette, accent, or unspoken assumption rather than confrontation — the insult delivered as a compliment, the exclusion dressed as tradition. Its power lies precisely in not looking like violence at all. These essays trace how cinema makes this invisible domination visible.' where canon_slug = 'symbolic-violence';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Haraway''s cyborg figure collapses the boundaries between human, animal, and machine, offering a way to imagine identity outside rigid categories of nature, gender, and origin. Science fiction cinema has run with this literally — bodies fused with technology that trouble what counts as authentic or natural — but the concept extends to any film that treats hybridity as liberation rather than monstrosity. The cyborg isn''t a warning in Haraway''s original sense; it''s a proposal. The list ahead follows how cinema takes up, or resists, that proposal.' where canon_slug = 'the-cyborg';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s death drive posits a force within the psyche pulling toward stasis, repetition, and ultimately self-dissolution, operating alongside and against the pleasure principle. Films whose characters court self-destruction with a kind of magnetic inevitability — return to a doomed love, compulsive repetition of a fatal pattern, a slow drift toward annihilation that feels chosen rather than accidental — dramatize this drive with unsettling clarity. It''s the theory that explains why some characters seem to want the worst outcome. These readings track that undertow through cinema''s most self-destructive protagonists.' where canon_slug = 'the-death-drive';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Barthes''s death of the author argues that a text''s meaning is not anchored in its creator''s intentions but activated by readers, freeing interpretation from biographical authority. Cinema complicates and extends this claim through collaborative authorship, adaptation, and remix culture, where a film''s meaning circulates independently of any single director''s stated purpose — sometimes against it. The moment audiences begin arguing about "what the director really meant," this essay is already relevant, and arguably already refuted. The list below follows Barthes''s polemic into film theory''s ongoing authorship debates.' where canon_slug = 'the-death-of-the-author';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Jameson''s political unconscious insists that all cultural texts, however seemingly apolitical, encode the ideological contradictions of their historical moment beneath their surface content. Genre films are Jameson''s favorite hunting ground: the monster movie that displaces class anxiety, the disaster film that stages collective fears about systemic collapse, the romance that resolves social tension through a wedding. Reading a film this way means treating plot as symptom rather than simply story. These essays apply that method to cinema''s buried historical anxieties.' where canon_slug = 'the-political-unconscious';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Lefebvre''s production of space argues that space is not a neutral container but actively produced through social, economic, and political practice — space as verb, not noun. Films set in planned cities, contested territories, or rapidly redeveloped neighborhoods make this production visible, showing how power literally shapes concrete, sightlines, and access. A film''s setting, in this reading, is never just backdrop; it''s an argument about who gets to make space and for whom. The readings ahead trace that argument across cinema''s built environments.' where canon_slug = 'the-production-of-space';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Winnicott''s transitional object — the blanket, the toy — occupies a psychological space between the infant''s inner world and external reality, easing the passage toward independence without denying the comfort of attachment. Films that fixate on a single object carried through trauma, war, or loss often borrow this function, letting a physical thing hold together an identity that reality keeps threatening to dissolve. The object''s eventual loss or abandonment frequently marks the film''s true emotional climax. This list follows Winnicott''s concept into cinema''s most freighted props.' where canon_slug = 'the-transitional-object';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Durkheim''s anomie describes a state of normlessness that follows the breakdown of social regulation, leaving individuals unmoored from the shared values that once gave their lives coherence. Films set amid rapid modernization, economic collapse, or the death of tradition often stage this condition through characters who drift without direction, unsure what rules still apply. The disorientation isn''t personal failure so much as a symptom of a society that has stopped supplying meaning. These readings trace anomie''s presence across cinema''s stories of social collapse.' where canon_slug = 'anomie';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Agamben''s bare life, drawn from the Roman figure of homo sacer, names a form of existence stripped of political protection — a life that can be killed without it counting as murder, existing outside the law''s care while remaining subject to its power. Films about refugees, detention camps, or stateless figures frequently dramatize exactly this exposed condition, where legal status determines the difference between a person and a body. The concept explains why some cinematic deaths register as invisible even when shown on screen. The list below follows Agamben''s theory through cinema''s zones of exception.' where canon_slug = 'bare-life-homo-sacer';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Hobbes''s bellum omnium contra omnes, the war of all against all, imagines a state of nature where no authority restrains competition, turning survival into perpetual conflict absent a sovereign to enforce peace. Post-apocalyptic and lawless-frontier films stage this thought experiment almost literally, stripping away institutions to watch what emerges when every character must fend entirely for themselves. The genre''s fascination with makeshift social contracts — alliances, codes, new hierarchies — is Hobbes''s solution playing out in real time. These readings trace that Hobbesian logic across cinema''s stateless worlds.' where canon_slug = 'bellum-omnium-contra-omnes';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Freud''s castration anxiety describes a foundational fear organized around loss and lack, later expanded by film theorists to explain cinema''s recurring fixation on cutting, wounding, and bodily vulnerability as sites of psychic dread. Horror and thriller imagery — blades, amputations, symbolic emasculation of powerful men — often externalizes an anxiety the theory locates deep in psychosexual development. Whether or not viewers buy the Freudian framework, the persistence of these images demands some explanation. The essays ahead test castration anxiety against cinema''s most visceral fears.' where canon_slug = 'castration-anxiety';
update sm_concepts set intro = 'Schopenhauer''s denial of the will-to-live proposes that suffering is relieved not by satisfying desire but by renouncing the will that generates desire in the first place, a kind of philosophical asceticism aimed at escaping endless striving. Films that end in renunciation rather than triumph — characters who walk away from love, ambition, or vengeance not out of defeat but clarity — often stage this Schopenhauerian resolution, refusing the usual narrative reward structure. The stillness that follows can feel more like victory than any conventional ending. This list follows that renunciation through cinema''s quietest endings.' where canon_slug = 'denial-of-the-will-to-live';

select count(*) as rows_with_intro, count(distinct canon_slug) as concepts_with_intro from sm_concepts where intro is not null;